## Summary

The [bindings docs](https://docs.deno.com/runtime/desktop/bindings/) state "Uint8Array: supported, for passing binary data" and "Stick to plain data and Uint8Array on both sides", and the type system agrees (`BrowserWindowLeaf` in `lib.deno.desktop.d.ts` includes `Uint8Array`; a handler returning `Promise<Uint8Array>` passes `BrowserWindowSerializableCheck` and compiles cleanly).

At runtime, however, a binding handler returning a `Uint8Array` always rejects webview-side with:

```
TypeError: invalid type: byte array, expected any valid JSON value
```

This makes binary data effectively untransportable through bindings in the Deno→webview direction.

## Versions tested

| deno | backend | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2.9.4 (stable, release, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu) | cef | FAIL |
| 2.9.5 (stable, release, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu) | cef (laufey v0.6.1) | FAIL |
| 2.9.5 (stable, release, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu) | webview (laufey v0.6.1) | FAIL |

OS: Linux (Wayland). A `ping` control binding returns `pong` in all configurations, so the bindings channel itself is healthy; only `Uint8Array` return values fail. Fails identically for freshly allocated arrays and subarray views.

## Repro

```ts
// repro.ts — deno desktop --hmr -A repro.ts
// The window shows the result; this script also prints it to the terminal
// via win.executeJs polling and then exits.
const handler = () =>
   new Response(
      `<!doctype html><pre>calling bindings.readBytes()…</pre>
      <script type="module">
         const out = document.querySelector("pre");
         let msg;
         try {
            const r = await bindings.readBytes();
            msg = "ok: " + r?.constructor?.name + " [" + Array.from(r) + "]";
         } catch (err) {
            msg = "FAIL: " + err.message;
         }
         const ping = await bindings.ping().catch((e) => "err:" + e.message);
         out.textContent = msg + " | ping=" + ping;
      </script>`,
      { headers: { "content-type": "text/html" } }
   );

// Standard desktop serving plumbing: DENO_SERVE_ADDRESS is assigned by the runtime.
if (Deno.env.has("DENO_SERVE_ADDRESS")) Deno.serve(handler);
else Deno.serve({ port: 8000 }, handler);
const url = Deno.env.has("DENO_SERVE_ADDRESS")
   ? `http://127.0.0.1:${Deno.env.get("DENO_SERVE_ADDRESS")!.split(":").pop()}`
   : "http://127.0.0.1:8000";

const win = new Deno.BrowserWindow({ title: "uint8 bind repro", width: 480, height: 240 });
win.bind("readBytes", async () => new Uint8Array([1, 2, 3, 255]));
win.bind("ping", async () => "pong");
win.navigate(url);

for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
   await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 500));
   const env = await win
      .executeJs('document.querySelector("pre").textContent')
      .catch((e: unknown) => `executeJs err: ${e}`);
   const text = typeof env === "object" && env !== null && "value" in env ? env.value : env;
   if (typeof text === "string" && (text.startsWith("ok:") || text.startsWith("FAIL:"))) {
      console.log("page says:", text);
      Deno.exit(0);
   }
}
console.log("page never updated");
Deno.exit(2);
```

Run: `deno desktop --hmr -A repro.ts`

**Expected** (per docs and types):

```
page says: ok: Uint8Array [1,2,3,255] | ping=pong
```

**Actual**:

```
page says: FAIL: TypeError: invalid type: byte array, expected any valid JSON value | ping=pong
```

## The argument direction is degraded too

A `Uint8Array` passed webview→Deno does not survive either, though silently. With `win.bind("echo", async (v) => v)`:

```js
await bindings.echo(new Uint8Array([1, 2, 3, 255])); // resolves to a plain object: {"0":1,"1":2,"2":3,"3":255} — not a Uint8Array
```

So binary data is currently untransportable in both directions: returns reject, arguments arrive as plain objects (which is also catastrophically slow for larger buffers — a single 1 MiB `Uint8Array` argument took ~250 s to cross in one measurement).

