## Summary

When a `Deno.serve` handler returns a `Response` whose body is a stream and whose `content-length` header is smaller than the body actually produces, Deno sends the declared `content-length` in the response head and then writes zero body bytes.

This is a regression in **2.8.3**. Versions 2.3.1 through 2.8.2 truncate the body to the declared length.

## Repro

```ts
const payload = "x".repeat(5_000);

const stream = new ReadableStream({
  start(controller) {
    controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode(payload));
    controller.close();
  },
});

const ac = new AbortController();
const server = Deno.serve({
  hostname: "127.0.0.1",
  port: 0,
  signal: ac.signal,
  onListen() {},
  onError(e) {
    console.log("onError fired:", e);
    return new Response("error", { status: 500 });
  },
  handler: () =>
    new Response(stream, { headers: { "content-length": "70" } }),
});

const port = (server.addr as Deno.NetAddr).port;
const conn = await Deno.connect({ hostname: "127.0.0.1", port });
await conn.write(
  new TextEncoder().encode(
    "GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: 127.0.0.1\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n",
  ),
);

const parts: number[] = [];
const buf = new Uint8Array(65536);
while (true) {
  const n = await conn.read(buf);
  if (n === null) break;
  parts.push(...buf.slice(0, n));
}

const raw = new TextDecoder("latin1").decode(new Uint8Array(parts));
const [head, ...rest] = raw.split("\r\n\r\n");

console.log(`deno ${Deno.version.deno}`);
console.log(head);
console.log(`--- body bytes received: ${rest.join("\r\n\r\n").length} ---`);

try {
  conn.close();
} catch { /* closed by peer */ }
ac.abort();
await server.finished;
```

Run with `deno run --allow-all repro.ts`.

## Results

| version | body bytes received |
| --- | --- |
| 2.3.1 | 70 |
| 2.4.5 | 70 |
| 2.5.7 | 70 |
| 2.6.10 | 70 |
| 2.7.14 | 70 |
| 2.8.2 | 70 |
| **2.8.3** | **0** |
| **2.9.3** | **0** |

The response head is identical in every case:

```
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
content-length: 70
connection: close
```

## Expected

I think we should do one of these - and we could even make it a configuration option to choose which approach is taken:

1. Throw, so `onError` fires and the failure is visible.
2. Ignore the supplied `content-length` and frame the response with the bytes actually produced (chunked, or a corrected length).
3. Truncate to the declared length, as 2.8.2 and earlier did.

## Note: sized bodies take a different path

With a string body instead of a stream, and the same lying header, 2.8.3+ silently **corrects** `content-length` to the true length and sends the whole body:

```ts
new Response("x".repeat(5_000), { headers: { "content-length": "70" } })
```

| version | header sent | body bytes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2.3.1 - 2.8.2 | `content-length: 70` | 5000 |
| 2.8.3 - 2.9.3 | `content-length: 5000` | 5000 |

The sized path learned to distrust the supplied header in the same release where the stream path started emitting nothing and it took the option 2 approach from above.

## Versions

Reproduced on `deno 2.8.3` and `deno 2.9.3` (aarch64-apple-darwin). Last good version is `2.8.2`.

### Version

- deno 2.9.1 (stable, release, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)
